[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I verified end-to-end data-plane, control-plane, and link health across the CLOS fabric. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1, multiple ping runs showed 0% loss and low, stable RTTs in both directions (25 packets each, ~1.3 ms avg). Host-to-gateway pings were also lossless on both subnets. Path MTU is healthy: DF pings at 1472-byte payload succeeded end-to-end. On the control plane, all EBGP sessions are Established and stable on every router (leafs, spines, super-spine). Each neighbor shows “Connections established 1; dropped 0” with several minutes of uptime and proper prefix exchange; route tables on leafs and spines contain the expected /24s with correct next hops. Links report up/10G/full duplex with no carrier flaps, and tc/if counters show no drops or errors.

Mechanism: Because the fabric is forwarding normally and BGP adjacencies are steady, there is no evidence of routing churn or link instability that would cause session drops. The one “timeout” observed in a long ping was due to the test runner’s 10s command cap, not network loss. An iperf test failed with “Connection refused” because no server was listening on pc_0_1—an application-level condition, not a network fault.

Localization: No device or interface shows abnormalities.

Root cause: No network-side issue present at this time.

Corrective fix: No changes required. If intermittent issues persist, check application/service availability on the endpoints and consider enabling BGP multipath (maximum-paths) on leafs for added path redundancy, though it’s not needed to resolve any current fault. 
=== END ===